static const LearnSection kLook[8][11] = {
  {
    {"big", "🔎 The big picture",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">This is a map of our <b>Solar System</b>: the Sun, eight planets, dwarf planets, moons, belts of rocks and comets. Where each one is drawn is where it really is on the date at the top left of the picture.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">You are in the <b>2D view</b>: you look straight down from above, on the north side of the Solar System. Seen from there, every planet goes round <b>counter-clockwise</b>.</p>\n      <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">{WHEN}</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Nothing is selected yet. Tap a planet, moon, belt or comet on the picture (or a tile under Go to) to get its facts card.</div></div>"},
    {"see", "👀 What is each thing on the picture?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Coloured balls with names</b> are the Sun, the planets, the dwarf planets and (when you zoom in) the moons. The faint line behind a planet is the path it has just travelled.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Grey circles with “AU”</b> are distance rings. <b>1 AU</b> is the distance from the Earth to the Sun, about 150 million km. The ring marked 20 AU is 20 times as far from the Sun as we are. Light needs about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to cross 1 AU.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The faint green band</b> is the <b>habitable zone</b>: the range of distances where a planet’s surface could be warm enough for liquid water. For the Sun this lab uses about 0.95 to 1.67 AU. It is a rough guide, and scientists give different numbers.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Clouds of tiny dots</b> are the belts: the asteroid belt (between Mars and Jupiter), Jupiter’s Trojans, the Kuiper belt beyond Neptune, and far out the Oort cloud.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Dotted coloured lines</b> are the orbits of the six famous comets. A comet has a glowing head, and near the Sun two tails.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Small open circles</b> around a body mean “it is here, but too small to see at this zoom”. Zoom in and the ball grows out of the circle.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The bar at the bottom left</b> is the scale bar: how long a round distance is at this zoom, and how long light needs to cross it. <b>The small arrows at the bottom right</b> show the directions: x and y lie in t"
        "he flat plane of the orbits, z points straight up out of it.</p>"},
    {"sky", "🌌 The stars and the far-away things",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>The stars in the background</b> (the <b>Soft</b> setting keeps them dim while you look at the planets and brightens them as you zoom out; <b>Full</b> keeps them bright) are real: the 2,874 brightest ones (about what your eyes see in a dark place), each in its true direction and true colour, with a soft band for the flat disc of our galaxy, the Milky Way (its real plane, drawn as a simple glow, not a photograph). They are so far away that they do not move when you zoom or follow something. They only move when you turn the picture in the 3D view (in 2D you look straight down, so you see the half of the sky below the Solar System).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Constellations</b> (in the Sky card) draws the lines people drew between stars long ago. The stars of a constellation are usually not near each other: they only look that way from here.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Far-away things:</b> zoom out a very long way (or tap a tile under Far away). The <b>white rings with a coloured dot</b> are real stars at their real direction and distance, like Alpha Centauri, the nearest star system, 4.3 light years away. The distance rings count light years too. The <b>blue rings</b> are the Pleiades and the Orion Nebula, and the <b>ring with a black dot</b> is Sagittarius A*, the black hole at the centre of our galaxy, 27,000 light years away (the Spacetime Lab shows what a black hole like it does). The <b>yellow rings</b> are Voyager 1 and Voyager 2, the two spacecraft t"
        "hat have left the Sun’s wind. Tap any of them for facts.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>What is to scale:</b> the directions and distances. The dots are markers, not sizes: at this scale even the Sun is less than a pixel. The Voyagers are at their real place on 30 Sep 2026 and then move along a straight line at their real speed, which is very close but not exact. Stars move slowly through space too, and that is ignored.</p>"},
    {"pink", "🎯 What is the pink ring?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>pink ring</b> means “look here”. It appears around whatever you selected, either by tapping it or with a Go to tile. It has a dark edge so you can spot it on any background. Tap an empty spot on the picture to close the card and clear the selection.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">The <b>Follow</b> button keeps the selected thing in the middle while time runs. That is how you can watch a moon go round its planet, or stay with a comet as it swings past the Sun.</p>"},
    {"spin", "🌍 Do the planets really spin?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Yes, at their real speed and leaning the real way.</b> A dashed line through each planet is its <b>axis</b>, and <b>N</b> marks the end it turns counter-clockwise around (the right-hand rule, like Earth’s North Pole). The directions and turning speeds come from the IAU (the body that names and measures the sky).</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Earth</b> shows its real coastlines, turned the way Earth really is on the date at the top left (within about half a degree). <b>The other planets</b> are plain-coloured with a grid (a line every 30° of latitude and longitude, a brighter equator, and one bright <b>0° meridian</b>, the line the IAU calls zero longitude) so you can see them turn. Their real cloud bands and spots are not drawn.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Leans:</b> Earth 23.4°, Mars 25.2°, Saturn 26.7°, Neptune 28.4°, Jupiter only 3.1°. <b>Uranus</b> lies on its side (97.8°). <b>Venus</b> is upside down (177.4°) and turns backwards: one turn takes 243 days, longer than its 225-day year. Saturn’s rings lie in its equator, so they tilt with it.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To watch one turn:</b> tap a planet, then <b>⏱ Slow time to watch it turn</b> (one turn takes about 24 seconds). At faster Time speeds a planet would turn more than once a second, so it is held still instead, and its card says so.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> the planets are drawn as perfect balls (real Jupiter and Saturn are squashed by about 6% and 10%). Earth’s turn ign"
        "ores tiny wobbles (a fraction of a degree). The Sun and the moons do not get a real spin yet.</p>"},
    {"trip", "🚀 Journey and jumping to a date",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>🚀 Journey</b> (in the Journey and dates card) zooms out by itself: Earth, the Moon’s orbit, Earth’s orbit, all the planets, Voyager 1, the Oort cloud, the nearest stars, the Pleiades and Orion Nebula, and the centre of our galaxy, with a caption at each stop. It runs at 1 second = 1 hour so you can see Earth turn, and your own Time speed comes back at the end. Touch the picture, the zoom or any tile to stop it.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Jump to a date</b> puts every planet, dwarf planet, comet and belt where it really was or will be on any date from 1800 to 2100 (Greenwich time), using NASA/JPL’s orbit numbers, which are made for 1800–2050 (after 2050 the places are extended and get slowly less exact). Try your birthday. Check: on 27 Aug 2003 Mars is 55.8 million km from Earth, as NASA says, and on 21 Dec 2020 Jupiter and Saturn are 0.1° apart.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Not real on a new date:</b> where each moon is (they start at made-up places), and the Voyagers, which only show from 1990 when both were flying in straight lines.</p>"},
    {"time", "⏱ What does Time speed mean?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">The Time speed slider decides how fast the lab’s time runs. It is set to <b>{TSPEED}</b> right now: that many days (or seconds) of lab time go by for every real second you watch.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">The planets need very different times for one lap: Mercury about 88 days, Earth one year, Neptune about 165 years. So a speed that shows the outer planets moving is far too fast for Mercury, and a speed that lets you watch a moon is far too slow for Neptune. The date at the top left counts the lab’s time. <b>☉ Reset to today</b> puts everything back where it is today.</p>"},
    {"scale", "📏 What is to scale, and what is not",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>To scale:</b> the distances, and the planets’ orbits. With <b>True scale</b> on, the planets, moons and the Sun are also drawn at their real size next to those distances. That is why the Sun is only a small ball, and the planets are far too small to see when you look at the whole Solar System. If you zoom in a lot, you will see how big the empty spaces really are.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Made up to be seen:</b> the colours of the orbits and dots, the tails of the comets (their real length changes, and they only grow near the Sun), and the moons’ orbits. Moons are so close to their planet that in a true picture they would hide inside it, so their orbits are drawn much wider than real until you zoom far in. The card of the moon says how many times too wide.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Stand-ins:</b> the belts are drawn with a few thousand dots. In reality the asteroid belt has millions of rocks wider than 1 km, and the Oort cloud is thought to hold trillions of icy bodies that nobody has seen directly.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Simplified:</b> in this lab each planet feels only the Sun’s pull, not the pull of the other planets. Over a few years that is very close to the real thing, but if you run time for centuries, the planets slowly drift away from where they would really be.</p>"},
    {"bary", "⚖️ Why does the Sun wobble?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">The planets do not really orbit the <i>centre of the Sun</i>. The Sun and everything around it circle their common balance point, the <b>barycentre</b>. Because the Sun is so heavy, that point is always very close to it, but it is not exactly at its centre.</p>\n      <div class=\"lrnCard\"><div class=\"lrnBig\">Right now the balance point is about <b>{BARY} Sun radii</b> from the Sun’s centre.</div><div class=\"lrnSm\">Over the years it wanders from inside the Sun to about 2 Sun radii out. Most of the pull comes from Jupiter, the heaviest planet. The whole picture is drawn around the barycentre, so the Sun is the one that wobbles. Tap the <b>Barycentre</b> tile under Go to to look at it.</div></div>"},
    {"comet", "☄️ What is a comet, and why the tails?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\">A comet is a ball of ice and dust, usually only a few kilometres across. Its orbit is a long, stretched ellipse. Far from the Sun it is frozen, dark and has no tail.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">Near the Sun, the ice turns to gas and blows off, and the comet grows a glowing cloud and <b>two tails</b>: a thin, straight <b>blue gas tail</b> and a wider, curved <b>yellow dust tail</b>. Both point <b>away from the Sun</b>, no matter which way the comet is moving. The push comes from sunlight and the solar wind, so on the way out a comet flies tail first.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">Halley’s Comet goes round the Sun backwards compared with the planets, and is back about every 76 years (next in 2061).</p>"},
    {"move", "🧭 How do I move around?",
        "<p class=\"lrnP\"><b>Zoom</b> with the scroll wheel, a pinch, or the Zoom slider. <b>Move the view</b> by dragging. <b>Tap</b> anything for its facts card. The <b>Go to</b> tiles fly you to a planet, moon, comet or belt and keep your Time speed as you set it.</p>\n      <p class=\"lrnP\">Lost? <b>↺ Reset view</b> brings back the whole picture.</p>"},
  },
